section.p:
Through this section, κ$\kappa$ will denote a regular uncountable cardinal. Our main goal is to clarify, for a given class of modules K$\mathcal {K}$, the relationship between K$\mathcal {K}$ being κ$\kappa$‐deconstructible and K$\mathcal {K}$ being bounded in κ$\kappa$. As noted in Section 2, any (κ,κ)$(\kappa,\kappa)$‐cofinal class K$\mathcal {K}$ is bounded in κ$\kappa$. In fact the former property seems to be strictly stronger than the latter, and they both follow from κ$\kappa$‐deconstructibility. Lemma 11 provides some important scenarios where these concepts are equivalent. Combining this lemma with the results of Bagaria and Magidor from Section 4 allows us to prove the main results mentioned in the introduction, namely,

(1)that the Maximum Deconstructibility principle introduced in [6] entails the existence of large cardinals;
(2)that in the absence of ω1$\omega _1$‐strongly compact cardinals, the class ⊥0Z${}^{\perp _0} \mathbb {Z}$ is a covering class (cf. Lemma 6) that is not deconstructible.


11
Lemma

Suppose κ$\kappa$ is a regular uncountable cardinal and R$R$ is a ring of size less than κ$\kappa$, and K$\mathcal {K}$ is a class of R$R$‐modules. Consider the following statements:

(a)K$\mathcal {K}$ is κ$\kappa$‐deconstructible;
(b)K$\mathcal {K}$ is (κ,κ)$(\kappa,\kappa)$‐cofinal and closed under transfinite extensions;
(c)K$\mathcal {K}$ is bounded by κ$\kappa$ and closed under transfinite extensions.

Then:

(1)(a) ⇒$\Rightarrow$ (b) ⇒$\Rightarrow$ (c);
(2)if K$\mathcal {K}$ is closed under quotients, that is, if
A⊂B,A∈K,andB∈K⇒B/A∈K$$\begin{equation*} {\left(A \subset B, \ A \in \mathcal {K}, \text{ and } B \in \mathcal {K} \right)} \Rightarrow \ B/A \in \mathcal {K} \end{equation*}$$for all modules A$A$ and B$B$, then (a) and (b) are equivalent;
(3)if K$\mathcal {K}$ is closed under homomorphic images and colimits, then all three statements are equivalent.
